GALTZAGORRIAK MINIX - MINIX 2.0.0 FOR THE FLIPPER ZERO

Galtzagorriak MINIX is a small distribution of MINIX 2.0.0 for the Flipper
Zero, named after the galtzagorriak, the small, helpful spirits of Basque
mythology.  This tree is MINIX 2.0.0 ported to run bare-metal on the Flipper Zero
(STM32WB55, ARM Cortex-M4, 64 MHz, 256 KB RAM, no MMU), replacing the
Flipper firmware.  The root file system lives on the microSD card.  A QEMU
target (mps2-an386, also a Cortex-M4) runs the same system for testing.

A machine's tribute to a machine: this project quietly honours AmigaOS.
It was not the best system of its time, nor the worst, nor even the fastest,
but it always answered: the pointer moved and a window could be dragged even
while everything else had hung, and people loved it for that.  Galtzagorriak
tries to keep that spirit: the screen and the buttons answer first, whatever
the rest of the system is doing.

The original PC sources are still here and the PC-specific code is
untouched; the port adds a new machine type (MACHINE FLIPPER, CHIP ARM_CM).
The full design, status and notes are in flipper/PORTING.md.


WHAT WORKS (in QEMU and on a real Flipper Zero)

  - The kernel with TTY, clock, memory, system and SD-card (WINCHESTER)
    tasks, and the real MM and FS servers.
  - fork() without an MMU through "shadowing" (as on the Atari ST), exec of
    relocatable programs, signals, pipes.
  - Memory protection with the MPU: each user process can only touch its
    own memory; flash is read-only.
  - init, getty, login, the shell and about 80 standard commands (ls, cat,
    cp/mv/rm, ed, grep, sed, sort, tar, df, mkfs, ...).
  - Console on the Flipper's USB port (appears as /dev/ttyACM0 on the PC)
    and on the serial pins 13 TX / 14 RX (115200 8N1); QEMU: the terminal.
  - LCD boot messages, backlight, RGB LED, buttons.

Packages: "pkg install file.tar", "pkg remove name", "pkg list", "pkg
info name", "pkg files name".  Packages are made on the PC with
flipper/tools/mkpkg.py (see flipper/minix.mk, target pkgs).  To install
them from the PC: log in as root on the USB console, close the terminal,
then run  flipper/tools/flsend file.tar ...  (it types "pkg get" and sends
the files with a CRC check).

Planned: LCD terminal with an on-screen keyboard, and
the Flipper hardware (GPIO, LED/vibro/speaker, infrared, Sub-GHz radio,
NFC/RFID, iButton, Bluetooth LE) with a menu interface like the stock
firmware.  See flipper/PORTING.md for the milestones.


BUILDING

Needs arm-none-eabi-gcc, python3, and qemu-system-arm or dfu-util.

  cd flipper
  make minix disk          # QEMU: kernel image + disk image
  make minix-run           # boot it in QEMU; log in as root (no password)

  make BOARD=flipper minix disk
                           # Flipper: build/flipper/minix/minix.bin (flash)
                           #          build/flipper/minix/fs.img   (SD card)

  make INIT=m2 minix minix-run
                           # instead of the real init: self-test, exits QEMU


INSTALLING ON THE FLIPPER

The full guide (backup, first boot, updates, recovery, back to stock) is
docs/manual/install.md.  In short:

1. SD card: write the card image to the WHOLE card (this erases it):

     make BOARD=flipper minix card
     sudo dd if=build/flipper/minix/card.img of=/dev/sdX bs=1M conv=fsync

   It holds a partition table and the 32 MB root file system.  On the first
   boot /etc/setup adds /usr (2 GB), a 64 MB swap area and /home (the rest,
   up to 4 GB) and reboots once; this takes a minute or two.

2. Flash: hold LEFT + BACK until the Flipper resets, release BACK and keep
   LEFT held to enter DFU mode, then

     make BOARD=flipper minix-flash

   Every MINIX image enters DFU the same way.  If an image is broken, use
   the hardware combination LEFT + BACK + OK, then release BACK and LEFT.
   The stock firmware can always be restored with qFlipper's "Repair".

3. Connect the Flipper by USB and open the serial port, e.g.
     screen /dev/ttyACM0
   Log in as root (no password).  Your user needs access to the port
   (group dialout).


LAYOUT

  flipper/            build system, HAL (board support), linker scripts,
                      host tools, root file system prototype, PORTING.md
  src/kernel/arm/     ARM entry/exit, interrupts, MPU, SD driver, console,
                      shadowing
  src/lib/arm/        ARM C run-time pieces of the C library
  include/            shared headers (ARM additions marked CHIP == ARM_CM)

